  nonce: 984d  # 984d
  message: "PROGRESS (Category B, within-package) merged 041e56d29c. In-tree submodule reads (import pkg; pkg.sub where sub is a first-party module) now resolve to the submodule's file node via a references edge (module_to_file_id threaded into _extract_edges -> _emit_module_attr_refs; exact dotted match, taint-safe, shadow-guarded). BEHAVIORAL: self-corpus workspace-prefixed external_symbol nodes 18 -> 13. REMAINING (stays todo_hard; INV-nuzas stays violated — NOT closed): (1) cross-PACKAGE submodule reads (hypergumbo_lang_extended1.prisma._analyzer, hypergumbo_lang_mainstream.rust._analyzer) still phantom — analyze() builds module_to_file_id PER-PACKAGE, so resolving these needs a GLOBAL cross-package module map (architectural); (2) Category D stdlib re-exports (cli.shutil, store.datetime) need cross-module import info (the receiver module's own imports), and the serve._ws_clients.copy case needs dispatch-class handling; (3) Category E bash:hypergumbo needs a cross-language console-script bridge OR a human-approved 9th carve-out. The 8 documented carve-outs (5 scip_pb2 + 3 dunders) remain the intended INV-nuzas acceptance-property exception once the resolvable residual clears."  # 984d
- op: discuss  # 2003
  at: '2026-07-14T04:11:34Z'  # 2003
  by: agent  # 2003
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 2003
  clock: 3  # 2003
  nonce: '2003'  # 2003
  message: "CORRECTION (2026-07-14): the 2026-07-13 note here claiming 'analyze() builds module_to_file_id PER-PACKAGE, so resolving these needs a GLOBAL cross-package module map (architectural)' is CODE-FALSIFIED. analyze_python(repo_root) runs once for the whole repo (py.py:5273); _detect_source_roots finds every package src/ (py.py:1814-1872); module_to_file_id is built from ALL packages' files (py.py:5405-5413) — the map is ALREADY global. The remaining Category-B _analyzer phantoms are NOT a missing-global-map issue: the module_attr_ref retarget (py.py:3613) is already module-scoped and the variable is registered (py.py:5335); the phantom persists via the INV-fahub shadow guard treating a method-local 'from pkg import submodule as Z' as a value-shadow (py.py:3408-3410 / 3612). Full analysis + residual breakdown on INV-nuzas (2026-07-14 record-correction). Do not act on the 'architectural global map' framing."  # 2003
- op: discuss  # 9639
  at: '2026-07-14T08:26:23Z'  # 9639
  by: agent  # 9639
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 9639
  clock: 4  # 9639
  nonce: '9639'  # 9639
  message: "CATEGORY B COMPLETE + CORRECTION (2026-07-14, PR #640, dev @ c0a40ff689). The remaining Category-B nodes rust._analyzer and prisma._analyzer are RESOLVED; Category B is now fully closed (the other two, hypergumbo_core.linkers and rust_analyzer.analyzer, landed earlier in 041e56d29c).\n\nCORRECTION of my 2026-07-13 note: I mischaracterized rust._analyzer/prisma._analyzer as \"cross-PACKAGE submodule reads\" needing \"a GLOBAL cross-package module map (architectural)\". WRONG on both counts. (a) They are WITHIN-package: test_rust.py + rust.py both live in hypergumbo-lang-mainstream; test_prisma.py + prisma.py both in extended1. (b) They are module_attr_ref reads of a module-level kind=variable (_analyzer), NOT submodule reads. Also: module_to_file_id is in fact built GLOBAL across all packages (not per-package as that note stated) — but the _analyzer fix did not need it anyway; the retarget resolves via global_symbols[(module, name)].\n\nREAL CAUSE: module_imports is FILE-scoped (built by ast.walk over the whole tree), so a sibling test method's plain `import hypergumbo_lang_mainstream.rust as rust_module` pollutes the file-level map; the reading method's own `from hypergumbo_lang_mainstream import rust as rust_module` was then added to the INV-fahub shadow set UNCONDITIONALLY (_collect_local_bindings ImportFrom branch), so the module_attr_ref retarget guard suppressed the (correct) resolution and emitted a workspace-prefixed external phantom.\n\nFIX (PR #640, py.py _collect_local_bindings): on the retarget path (include_import_aliases=False), exclude a from-import whose absolute (level==0) target equals the same name's module_imports binding — a co-referent module alias, not a value shadow. Per-alias exact (derived from the actual AST node); a genuine value rebind (`from pkg import CONST as m`), a later `m = ...` reassignment (re-added via the Store branch), and a different-module collision all still shadow (never a confidently-wrong resolved edge); relative co-referent imports stay phantom (safe miss). ~15 lines. NO global cross-package map was needed.\n\nBEHAVIORAL EVIDENCE (whole-repo self-analysis, dev @ c0a40ff689): Category-B `<module>._analyzer:external_symbol` phantoms = 0 (was 2); the two test_rust.py reads now emit is_resolved=True references to the real rust.py _analyzer variable. Total workspace-prefixed external_symbol phantoms now = 12 = 8 documented carve-outs (5 scip_pb2 + 3 dunders) + Category D x3 + Category E x1. So the ONLY statically-resolvable residual left is D + E.\n\nCATEGORY D (corrected mechanism, still PENDING — this sub-WI is the follow-up tracker): cli.shutil + store.datetime (module_attr_ref) + serve._ws_clients:copy (call). A stdlib/BUILTIN referenced THROUGH an in-tree module gets the workspace module path. _emit_module_attr_refs._emit_one falls through to qname=f'{real_module}.{attr}' keyed on the workspace module when attr is neither an in-tree variable/function (WI-huhum retarget) nor an in-tree submodule (module_to_file_id); it never consults the in-tree module's OWN import table (store's `import datetime` -> stdlib datetime). The call phantom is the WI-zigah multi-segment handler (no in-tree guard), which treats the module-level SET var _ws_clients as a submodule and builtin set.copy as an external function. CORRECTION: serve._ws_clients.copy is set.copy() — a BUILTIN METHOD, not the stdlib `copy` MODULE — so a naive \"relabel to stdlib\" would MINT a new mislabel; it needs suppression / references-retarget. FIX SHAPE: thread each in-tree module's resolved import map into the two emitters so a reference-through-an-in-tree-module relabels to its true target; for the call case, resolve the intermediate segment as a module-level symbol FIRST. This is a REAL bug but NOT an ADR-0041/0037 violation (ADR-0041 explicitly accepts phantom workspace twins as tier-3 externals \"until the workspace-resolution fix lands\"; ADR-0037 governs resolution-state coherence, not module-name accuracy). Medium effort/risk; the shared WI-huhum/WI-zigah paths also serve legit stdlib dotted chains (urllib.request.urlopen) + third-party re-exports, so tag awaits_bakeoff_validation when landed. All 3 are test-file-reached (consumer views _is_test_path-filtered), so consumer impact is low.\n\nCATEGORY E (bash:hypergumbo): the hypergumbo CLI IS genuinely external to a bash script; this is a cosmetic acceptance-property match (the id contains \"hypergumbo\"). Low value; a candidate for a 9th documented carve-out (like the scip_pb2 / dunder carve-outs) rather than a code fix."  # 9639
